V(DCM, IO_A,
  "A 64-year-old woman with dilated cardiomyopathy has breathlessness on exertion and peripheral edema. On examination her neck veins distend when firm pressure is held over her right upper abdomen, a sign called hepatojugular reflux (neck-vein distension with firm pressure over the liver). Which additional finding is expected with this degree of congestion?",
  [["Congestive enlargement of the liver",
    "Correct. Alongside hepatojugular reflux, the gastrointestinal findings of dilated cardiomyopathy include congestive hepatomegaly and ascites. They reflect venous congestion, which also gives jugular vein distention, an enlarged liver and spleen, and dependent pitting edema like hers."],
   ["A small, soft, nonpalpable liver",
    "A small liver is the opposite of what congestion does: in dilated cardiomyopathy the gastrointestinal findings are congestive hepatomegaly, hepatojugular reflux and ascites, with the liver and spleen enlarged from venous congestion. Tell: hepatojugular reflux and an enlarged liver travel together."],
   ["A pulsatile mass in the epigastrium",
    "A pulsatile epigastric mass is not among the congestive findings. With hepatojugular reflux, the expected gastrointestinal findings of dilated cardiomyopathy are congestive hepatomegaly and ascites. Tell: venous congestion enlarges the liver and fills the abdomen with fluid; it does not create a pulsating mass."],
   ["Flank bruising from bleeding",
    "Flank bruising has nothing to do with venous congestion. Dilated cardiomyopathy with hepatojugular reflux is expected to show congestive hepatomegaly and ascites, along with dependent pitting edema. Tell: congestion causes swelling and enlargement, not bruising."]],
  C(33), "manifestation", "complication"),

V(DCM, IO_A,
  "A 56-year-old man with dilated cardiomyopathy has an ejection fraction of 25%, a wide left bundle branch block on his electrocardiogram and persistent symptoms despite medical therapy. His cardiologist plans a device that paces both ventricles and can also shock a dangerous rhythm. Which device is being planned?",
  [["Biventricular defibrillator",
    "Correct. A biventricular pacemaker is used for moderately severe left ventricular dysfunction, and a defibrillator for an ejection fraction below 35% after maximum therapy. The biventricular defibrillator combines both."],
   ["Single-lead atrial pacemaker",
    "A single atrial pacemaker does not pace both ventricles or shock arrhythmias. Combining both functions is the biventricular defibrillator used in dilated cardiomyopathy."],
   ["Left ventricular assist pump",
    "An assist device pumps blood from the left ventricle into the aorta as a bridge or destination therapy. It neither paces nor defibrillates the heart."],
   ["Loop recorder for monitoring",
    "A loop recorder only records the rhythm. The device that both paces the two ventricles and treats dangerous rhythms is the biventricular defibrillator."]],
  C(43), "escalation", "treatment"),

